  "marks": "One mark for each correct point up to 2 marks:\n- Aluminium can be recycled repeatedly\n- Recycling aluminium requires significantly less energy than producing it from bauxite ore\n- Aluminium recycling is economically viable and well established in the UK\n(Any two for 2 marks)"
 },
 {
  "text": "Describe the difference between how a thermoplastic and a thermosetting plastic behave at end of life.",
  "type": "3 marks — Describe",
  "marks": "One mark for each distinct point up to 3 marks:\n- Thermoplastics soften on heating and can be re-moulded and recycled\n- Thermosetting plastics have a cross-linked structure that does not re-melt when heated\n- Thermosetting plastics therefore cannot be recycled and must go to landfill or incineration\n(Other valid points accepted)"
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain why a manufacturer designing a new electric kettle should consider the embodied energy of the materials they select.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Secure: Two developed points linking high embodied energy to greater environmental cost at production, with reference to recyclability at end of life and how choosing lower-embodied-energy or highly recyclable materials reduces overall impact.\nDeveloping: States that embodied energy affects environmental impact with limited explanation of the mechanism or product application.\nEmerging: Vague reference to environmental impact without specific engineering content."
 },
 {
  "text": "A bicycle manufacturer wants to improve the sustainability of their product. Discuss the environmental issues the manufacturer should consider across the full life of the bicycle, from materials selection to end-of-life disposal.",
  "type": "9 marks — Discuss / Analyse (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering: Multiple stages of the product lifecycle considered (materials/embodied energy, manufacture waste, transport emissions, use-phase energy, disposal and recycling options for steel, aluminium or composite frame), with trade-offs weighed and a reasoned conclusion about the most significant environmental priority. Engineering vocabulary used precisely.\nSecure: At least three lifecycle stages addressed with clear engineering explanation for each.\nDeveloping: Two stages considered with some engineering reasoning, or three stages considered at surface level without development.\nEmerging: One stage (typically disposal) discussed without considering others."
 }
]

